    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im having some issues with our sublimation heat press line. the colors are coming out really faded and not transferring to the fabric like they should. we checked the settings but they seem right. what should i be looking at for temp and pressure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Faded colors can be a real challenge, especially when it comes to sublimation heat pressing. It's a bit like trying to get a stubborn band of goblins to cooperate - you think you've got the right settings, but they just won't budge. \n\nIn this case, let's take a closer look at the temperature and pressure. You mentioned checking the settings, but I'd like to dig a bit deeper. Have you considered the type of fabric you're working with? Some fabrics, like cotton or polyester blends, can be more finicky when it comes to sublimation. It's possible that the heat press is having trouble penetrating the fabric, causing the colors to fade.\n\nWhen it comes to temperature, a good starting point is around 380-400°F (193-204°C). However, this can vary depending on the specific fabric and the type of ink you're using. It's also worth noting that some heat presses have a \"soak\" or \"pre-heat\" function that can help ensure the ink is fully dissolved and ready to transfer.\n\nAs for pressure, a medium to high pressure setting is usually recommended for sublimation. This will help ensure the ink is transferred evenly and prevents any air",
      "source": "target",
