    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait, what do you mean by goblins? anyway, back to the press. we are using 100% high-polyester fabric and our temp is locked at 400F. pressure is definitely high enough. could it be the timing or maybe something with the ink itself?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The goblins, yes, well, you see, I was just using them as a metaphor, a bit of a stretch, I know. It's just that sublimation can be a bit like navigating a treacherous goblin-infested cave - you think you've got the right path, but sometimes the colors just don't cooperate.\n\nNow, let's get back to the press. With 100% high-polyester fabric and a temperature of 400F, you're on the right track. Pressure is indeed a crucial factor, and it sounds like you're on the right side of that.\n\nTiming is a great point to consider. Sublimation ink can take a bit of time to fully transfer, especially if the fabric is thick or the ink is not fully dissolved. It's possible that the ink is not fully transferred before the press is turned off, resulting in faded colors.\n\nAs for the ink itself, it's possible that the ink is not suitable for high-polyester fabrics. Some inks are designed for specific types of fabrics, and high-polyester fabrics can be particularly finicky. You might consider switching to an ink that's specifically designed for polyester or using a different type of ink altogether.\n\nOne",
      "source": "target",
